  started doing that for a recent while  now so going forward we're probably not  going to see the headphone jack  anymore and personally I'm okay with  that because I actually enjoy the audio  quality through a Bluetooth connection  more than I enjoy the quality from a  cheaper nastier headphone jack another  big thing that we're gonna see the  removal of in the Samsung Galaxy lineup  is the fact that the Bigsby button will  be removed completely removing the big  speed button is a big winner for samsung  here and for consumers I know personally  I use a Samsung Galaxy device and I keep  tapping that Bigsby button all the time  and it comes on I know you can block it  turn it off but it's still a very  inconvenient button to have and  something that really should never have  been there in the first place a new  feature that we won't actually see but  it will be very very useful is the  simple fact that they're gonna double  the size of the fingerprint scanner  underneath the glass you're not gonna be  how to tap around trying to locate that  fingerprint scanner so much anymore and  it's gonna make unlocking your phone  even easier than it already is last but  not least we're expecting to see  affordable prices especially with the 5g  models I don't know about the rest of  the world but in Australia the telcos  are taking advantage of 5g and they're  taking advantage of consumers Telstra  for example is one of Australia's  biggest telcos and right now if you go  on their website you'll see that in mid  next year they're gonna start charging  an additional $15 for consumers to use  the 5g network now personally I think  that's a bit unfair and a bit of a rot  but I guess there's not much you can do  about it when they're the leading 5g  telco supply in Australia they could  charge whatever they want and consumers  more than likely are going to pay the  fees personally I know that in Australia  there is way too much land to cover with  the 5g network and we're still  